import { DocsPage, Demo, Code, ApiReference } from "../../shell.rx"
import { SortableHero } from "../../demos/sortable/hero.rx"
import { SortableTags } from "../../demos/sortable/tags.rx"
import { SortableBoard } from "../../demos/sortable/board.rx"
import { SortableIndicator } from "../../demos/sortable/indicator.rx"
import { SortableSaving } from "../../demos/sortable/saving.rx"

export default component Page() {
  <DocsPage title="Sortable" subtitle="A list whose items can be reordered by drag and drop, with the pointer or the keyboard.">
    <Demo source="sortable/hero"><SortableHero /></Demo>

    <h2 id="anatomy">Anatomy</h2>
    <p>Import the parts and put them together:</p>
    <Code file="sortable/anatomy.rx.txt" />

    <h2 id="platform">What the browser does</h2>
    <p>
      Less than for most families. All three engines ship the HTML Drag and Drop API (<code>draggable</code>,{" "}
      <code>dragstart</code>, <code>dragover</code>, <code>drop</code>), and it was tried first. Measured in
      Chromium, Firefox and WebKit, a mouse drag of a <code>draggable</code> element does reach a drop target, but
      once <code>dragstart</code> fires the page gets no more pointer events for that pointer (Chromium and Firefox
      send <code>pointercancel</code>, WebKit stops sending them), so the list can only react to{" "}
      <code>dragover</code> wherever the pointer happens to be. The drag image is the browser's, not the element, and
      there is no keyboard path at all. So the pointer path is the library's: pointer events with pointer capture, as
      Slider's drag is.
    </p>
    <p>
      The browser does provide the rest of what the drag stands on: <code>touch-action</code> on the handle so a touch
      drags instead of scrolling, the <code>{"<button>"}</code> that makes a handle focusable and pressable, and the
      CSS that animates the drag. Moving an element in the DOM blurs it in every engine if it has focus, so nothing
      moves in the DOM while an item is dragged: the dragged item and the items that make way for it are offset
      through two CSS variables, and the order changes once, on the drop. The handle you were using keeps focus
      through that change, and through an order that goes back because your server refused it.
    </p>
    <p>
      Same-document View Transitions also run in all three, and aren't used: with the offsets in CSS variables, your
      CSS animates the drag (<code>translate: var(--sortable-x) var(--sortable-y)</code> on the items and a{" "}
      <code>transition</code> on the ones making way), and only the dragged item has to follow the pointer.
    </p>
    <p>
      Without help, a mouse drag selects the text it passes over, in all three engines. While a press may become a
      drag, the list stops text selection, and the native drag of an image or link inside an item, until the
      release: there is no need for <code>user-select: none</code>.
    </p>
    <p>
      The library adds the order and its changes, the measuring that says where an item would land, scrolling a list
      that scrolls while the pointer is held near its edge, the keyboard path, the announcements, and moving items
      between lists.
    </p>
    <div class="note">
      <p>
        Base UI has no such component. The keyboard and screen reader behaviour follows React Aria's drag and drop
        for reorderable lists and the WAI-ARIA Authoring Practices: a keyboard way to do everything a pointer does,
        every step announced, and none of the deprecated <code>aria-grabbed</code> or <code>aria-dropeffect</code>.
        Sortable reorders and moves items between lists on one page. Dragging other things onto drop targets, or
        data in or out of the page (files, text, another window), is what the native API above does well: use{" "}
        <code>draggable</code> and <code>DataTransfer</code> for that. A list that wraps onto several rows (a grid)
        isn't supported: each list is measured along one axis. Touch input hasn't been tried on a real device: the long
        press is tested with dispatched events.
      </p>
    </div>

    <h2 id="examples">Examples</h2>

    <h3 id="keyboard">From the keyboard</h3>
    <p>
      Tab to a handle and press <kbd>Space</kbd> or <kbd>Enter</kbd> to pick its item up. The arrow keys along the
      list move it one place, <kbd>Home</kbd> and <kbd>End</kbd> to either end; <kbd>Space</kbd> or{" "}
      <kbd>Enter</kbd> drops it, and <kbd>Escape</kbd> puts it back where it was. Moving focus away mid-drag cancels
      it too. Each step is announced in an assertive live region beside the list: "Picked up Order paper, position 4
      of 5", "Order paper moved to position 2 of 5", "Dropped…", "Cancelled…". Handles carry{" "}
      <code>aria-roledescription="sortable"</code>, an <code>aria-describedby</code> that points at those
      instructions, and <code>aria-pressed</code> while their item is dragged.
    </p>
    <p>
      To translate the texts, pass <code>messages</code>: any of <code>pickedUp</code>, <code>moved</code>,{" "}
      <code>dropped</code> and <code>cancelled</code> (functions of the item's <code>label</code>,{" "}
      <code>position</code>, <code>total</code> and <code>listLabel</code>), <code>instructions</code> and{" "}
      <code>roleDescription</code>. An item's label is its <code>aria-label</code>, else its text without the
      handle's and its buttons'; the messages also get its <code>id</code>, to look a better name up yourself.
    </p>

    <h3 id="whole-item">The whole item as the handle</h3>
    <p>
      Leave <code>SortableHandle</code> out and the item is its own handle: it takes focus, is described by the
      instructions, and a press anywhere on it drags, except a press or a key on a control inside it. A touch drags
      after a long press, so a swipe across the list still scrolls it; a handle, with its{" "}
      <code>touch-action: none</code>, drags at once. This list is horizontal, so the left and right arrows move a
      tag (in a right-to-left page, the other way round).
    </p>
    <Demo source="sortable/tags"><SortableTags /></Demo>
    <p>
      It is also uncontrolled. The root can't reorder children it was given, so an uncontrolled list renders its items
      from the order in its context: the <code>Tags</code> component reads <code>use sortable</code> and loops over{" "}
      <code>value</code>.
    </p>

    <h3 id="groups">Moving items between lists</h3>
    <p>
      Put the lists in a <code>SortableGroup</code> and give each root a <code>listId</code>. The group owns every
      list's order, as an object from list id to item ids, and an item can be dragged into another list, or moved
      there from the keyboard with the arrows across the lists. The root under the pointer has{" "}
      <code>data-drop-target</code>; give an empty list a <code>min-height</code> so it can be dropped into.
    </p>
    <Demo source="sortable/board"><SortableBoard /></Demo>

    <h3 id="styling">Styling the drag</h3>
    <p>
      The items carry their offset in <code>--sortable-x</code> and <code>--sortable-y</code>, <code>0px</code> at
      rest. The dragged item has <code>data-dragging</code> (<code>pointer</code> or <code>keyboard</code>) and the
      items making way have <code>data-shifted</code>. Transition only those: the attributes go in the same change
      as the order, so at the drop nothing animates back, and a pointer drag is followed without lag. Raise the
      dragged item with <code>position: relative</code> and a <code>z-index</code>; in a group it stays in its own
      list's element until the drop, so don't clip the lists.
    </p>
    <p>
      For a drop indicator instead of a gap, leave the offsets unused and draw a line on the item with{" "}
      <code>data-drop-target</code>: <code>before</code> it, or <code>after</code> it at the end of the list.
    </p>
    <Demo source="sortable/indicator"><SortableIndicator /></Demo>

    <h3 id="saving">Saving to a server</h3>
    <p>
      <code>onValueChange</code> is called once per drop, never while an item is carried, so it can save. It is called
      in a change of its own: a handler that waits for its server never holds the list, which can be dragged again at
      once. Write it as an action with a <code>guess</code> and the new order shows at once and goes back by itself if
      the save fails; without a guess the list shows your order until you write the new one.
    </p>
    <Demo source="sortable/saving"><SortableSaving /></Demo>

    <h2 id="api-reference">API reference</h2>
    <ApiReference family="sortable" />
  </DocsPage>
}
